namespace gismo {

/** \defgroup System System module
\ingroup Modules

    This module provides the machinery that lets optional modules of \gismo be
    compiled into the library or loaded at run time as separate libraries.

\section System_what What a module is

    An optional module (the directory <tt>optional/\<name\></tt>, enabled
    through the CMake variable \c GISMO_OPTIONAL) is either compiled into
    libgismo (the default), or, when its name is also listed in the CMake
    variable \c GISMO_DYNAMIC_MODULES, built as a separate library that is
    loaded at run time. Code that reaches a module through a registry works
    the same in both modes.

\section System_entry Entry points

    Every module defines its entry points with the macro
    \c GISMO_MODULE_ENTRY(name, version) (gsModule.h), used once per module at
    namespace scope and followed by the body of the register function. The
    argument \c name is a bare identifier equal to the CMake name of the
    module, \c version is a string literal. The symbols have C linkage:

    - Dynamic mode (\c GISMO_MODULE_DYNAMIC, defined by
      \c gismo_add_module_library): the exported functions
      \c gismo_module_info and \c gismo_module_register.
    - Built-in mode: the function \c gismo_module_register_\<name\>, called
      from the file \c gsBuiltinModules.cpp generated in the build tree for
      every module that called \c gismo_add_builtin_module(\<name\>).

    The classes involved are:

    - gsModuleManager: process-wide singleton that registers the built-in
      modules and loads dynamic ones (gsModuleManager::load,
      gsModuleManager::loadAll, gsModuleManager::unload). It reports the
      modules it knows with gsModuleManager::has,
      gsModuleManager::isLoaded and gsModuleManager::modules, and the files
      it refused with gsModuleManager::rejected.
    - gsRegistry: key-to-factory map per base type, filled by the register
      function of a module (gsRegistry::add) and queried by users
      (gsRegistry::has, gsRegistry::make, gsRegistry::keys). The helper
      gsRegistryFactory makes a factory of a derived class. There is one
      registry per base type and process, instantiated in and exported from
      libgismo (\c gsRegistry_.cpp); a module adds to it and must not
      instantiate its own.
    - gsFileIo: reader and writer of a file format provided by a module,
      found with gsFileIo::find.

    gsFileData uses gsFileIo for extensions it has no native reader or writer
    for: \c gsFileData::read passes such an extension to gsFileIo::find, and
    \c gsFileData::save passes any extension other than \c xml and \c gz to it
    first; if the format can be written, that format is written, and no
    \c .xml file. If the writer fails, a warning is printed and nothing else
    is written.

    \code
    // Loading is automatic when an unknown extension is met ...
    gsFileData<> fd("surface.3dm");
    fd.save("out.3dm");

    // ... or explicit
    gsModuleManager::get().load("gsOpennurbs");
    \endcode

    The key of gsFileIo::find is the extension in lower case without the dot.
    When it is not registered, gsModuleManager::loadAll runs once and the
    lookup is repeated.

    The handshake of a dynamic module is the structure gismo_module_info_t
    (gsModuleInfo.h), checked by gsModuleManager::load. A module is rejected,
    with a warning, and listed by gsModuleManager::rejected, unless its
    \c abi_major equals \c GISMO_MAJOR, its \c abi_minor is not newer than
    \c GISMO_MINOR, and its \c coeff_type and \c index_type equal those of
    the host.

\section System_search Search order

    A module \c \<name\> is the file \c \<name\>.so (\c \<name\>.dll on
    Windows), without a \c lib prefix. The argument of gsModuleManager::load
    that contains a path separator is used as the path of the file; otherwise
    it is a module name, and the file is looked for in the directories of
    gsModuleManager::searchPaths, in this order:

    -# the entries of the environment variable \c GISMO_MODULE_PATH
       (separated by \c : , or by \c ; on Windows),
    -# the directories given to gsModuleManager::addSearchPath,
    -# the compiled-in directory \c GISMO_MODULE_DIR (the build tree),
    -# the compiled-in directory \c GISMO_MODULE_INSTALL_DIR (the install tree).

    Empty entries and repeated directories are skipped, and the first file
    found wins. \c GISMO_MODULE_DIR is a macro of the generated header
    \c gsModuleConfig.h, not an environment variable.
    gsModuleManager::loadAll tries every file with the module suffix in these
    directories, except files whose name starts with \c libgismo, and skips
    modules that are present or were already rejected.

\section System_build Building a module dynamically

    For the user: configure with the module in both \c GISMO_OPTIONAL and
    \c GISMO_DYNAMIC_MODULES. This needs \c GISMO_BUILD_LIB=ON; without it a
    warning is printed and the module is built into the library.

    \code{.sh}
    cmake . -DGISMO_BUILD_LIB=ON -DGISMO_OPTIONAL="gsOpennurbs" -DGISMO_DYNAMIC_MODULES=gsOpennurbs
    \endcode

    The module file is written to <tt>\<build\>/lib/gismo/modules</tt>
    (\c GISMO_MODULE_DIR) and installed to
    <tt>\<prefix\>/\<LIB_INSTALL_DIR\>/gismo/modules</tt>
    (\c GISMO_MODULE_INSTALL_DIR). The generated header \c gsModuleConfig.h is
    installed with the other headers of gsSystem.

    For the module author: the CMake functions are defined in
    \c cmake/gsModule.cmake.

    - <tt>gismo_add_module_library(\<name\> SOURCES \<files\> [LINK \<libs\>])</tt>
      builds \c \<name\> as a MODULE library linked to \c gismo, with
      \c GISMO_MODULE_DYNAMIC defined and hidden symbol visibility.
    - <tt>gismo_add_builtin_module(\<name\>)</tt> records that the object code
      of \c \<name\> is part of libgismo.
    - <tt>gismo_configure_modules()</tt> generates \c gsModuleConfig.h and
      \c gsBuiltinModules.cpp once all optional modules have been added.

    A module is a client of libgismo, which is built with hidden symbol
    visibility: it can call only the symbols that libgismo exports
    (\c GISMO_EXPORT).

    The module \c gsOpennurbs chooses the mode in its \c CMakeLists.txt:

    \code{.cmake}
    if(GSOPENNURBS_DYNAMIC)
      find_package(ZLIB QUIET)
      if(NOT ZLIB_FOUND)
        message(FATAL_ERROR "gsOpennurbs: dynamic module needs a system zlib")
      endif()
      set(GSOPENNURBS_LINK ${ZLIB_LIBRARIES})
      if ("x${CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER_ID}" STREQUAL "xMSVC")
        set(GSOPENNURBS_LINK ${GSOPENNURBS_LINK} Rpcrt4)
      endif()
      gismo_add_module_library(${PROJECT_NAME} SOURCES ${OPENNURBS_SOURCES} LINK ${GSOPENNURBS_LINK})
    else()
      add_library(${PROJECT_NAME} OBJECT ${OPENNURBS_SOURCES}) #STATIC
      #target_link_libraries(${PROJECT_NAME} Rpcrt4) # Note: linked with gismolib instead
    endif()
    \endcode

    and, for the built-in branch, calls \c gismo_add_builtin_module near the
    end of the file. \c GSOPENNURBS_DYNAMIC is set when \c gsOpennurbs is in
    \c GISMO_DYNAMIC_MODULES, except when \c GISMO_ZLIB_STATIC is set: then
    a warning is printed and the module is built into the library, because the
    prefixed zlib inside libgismo needs OpenNURBS.

\section System_format Adding a file format

    Derive a class from gsFileIo, override gsFileIo::canRead,
    gsFileIo::canWrite, gsFileIo::read and gsFileIo::write (they work on a
    \c gsXmlTree, the in-memory form of the \gismo xml file), and register a
    factory of it under the lower-case extension without the dot, in the body
    of \c GISMO_MODULE_ENTRY. Build the module with the CMake pattern of
    \ref System_build. No change to gsFileData is needed.

    The worked example is \c gsOpennurbs (extension \c 3dm), in
    \c optional/gsOpennurbs/gsOpenNurbsFile.cpp:

    \code
    GISMO_MODULE_ENTRY(gsOpennurbs, GISMO_VERSION)
    {
        gsRegistry<gsFileIo>::get().add("3dm", &gsRegistryFactory<gsFileIo, gsOpenNurbsFile>);
    }
    \endcode

    The 3dm writer writes \c gsBSpline curves and \c gsTensorBSpline<2>
    surfaces of geometric dimension 2 or 3; other geometry kinds (NURBS among
    them) are skipped with a warning.

\section System_limits Known limits

    - Dynamic mode is verified on Linux only; Windows and macOS are untested.
    - The manager and the registries are not thread-safe.
    - A module built dynamically has no \c \<name\>_ENABLED define, so code
      under <tt>\#ifdef \<name\>_ENABLED</tt> (for instance the 3dm parts of
      \c examples/fileIo_example.cpp) is compiled out in that mode.
    - Every object made by a factory of a module must be destroyed before the
      module is unloaded; gsFileData creates and destroys its gsFileIo inside
      one call.
    - The register function of a module should not load another module:
      registrations made after such a nested load are not attributed to the
      outer module (see gsModuleManager::registering).
    - gsModuleManager::unload returns false for a built-in module.

*/

}
